best:
And burn us up to quiet! Ah, we know
Too much here, not to know what’s best for peace;
We have too much light here, not to want more fire

beset:
With children of their own;
Their mother-hearts beset with fears,
Their lives bound up in tender lives;

bestowing:
Meanwhile I wait their leisure
And grace-bestowing pleasure,
I wait the happy man;

bestow'd:
With pensive mind I search the drear abode,
Where the great conqu'ror has his spoils bestow'd;
There where the offspring of six thousand years

bestir:
The quiet nonchalance of death
No daybreak can bestir;
The slow archangel's syllables

bested:
Within me, as my body in this mire;
My soul crawls dumb-struck, sore-bested and cowed.
As Sodom and Gomorrah scourged by fire,

bestial:
If that June-morning had not failed my hope,—
I’m not so bestial, to regret that day
This night,—this night, which still to you is fair;

bespangles:
Wrapped in its perfumes, the darkness is holding you;
Starlight bespangles the way of your dreams.
Chorus the nightingales, wistfully amorous;
